 ## Background
-The concept of interpolation in volume rendering originated with the need to reconstruct continuous signals from discrete voxel data. Early work by Westover (1990) introduced footprint evaluation for volume rendering, highlighting the importance of interpolation schemes in determining image quality [S3, S4, unofficial]. Levoy (1988) and Van Hook (1986) established ray-casting methods that relied on linear interpolation for isosurface detection and density estimation [S2, unofficial]. The distinction between nearest-neighbor (zeroth-order), linear (first-order), and cubic (higher-order) interpolation was formalized in early parallel volume rendering algorithms, noting the trade-off between computational cost (1, 8, or 64 samples in 3D) and visual quality [S2, unofficial]. The use of frequency-domain methods, such as the Shear-Warp factorization with sinc resampling, was later developed to address aliasing and blurring artifacts inherent in spatial-domain filters, particularly for large zoom factors [S1, S3, S4, unofficial].
+The concept of interpolation in volume rendering originated with the need to handle the discrete nature of voxel data in ray-casting algorithms. Early work by Levoy [S2, unofficial] established the trade-offs between nearest-neighbor, linear, and cubic interpolation for ray-casting, noting that nearest-neighbor leads to blockiness and aliasing, while higher-order methods improve quality at increased computational cost. The use of frequency domain methods, specifically sinc resampling, was introduced by Westover [S4, unofficial] to address the limitations of spatial domain filters, particularly for large magnification factors. The development of GPU-accelerated techniques, such as fast tri-cubic interpolation [S1, unofficial] and hardware interpolation approximation [S1, unofficial], further refined these methods to leverage modern graphics hardware for real-time rendering.
 
